1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,618
Scientists believe that a majority of the earth’s bird population migrate in some fashion

2
00:00:07,618 --> 00:00:08,080
or other.

3
00:00:08,080 --> 00:00:14,764
Some travel seasonally for relatively short distances, such as birds that move from their

4
00:00:14,764 --> 00:00:19,100
winter habitats in lowlands to mountain tops for the summers.

5
00:00:19,100 --> 00:00:27,885
Others, like the Arctic Tern, travel more than 25,000 miles seasonally between the northern

6
00:00:27,885 --> 00:00:29,850
and southern poles.

7
00:00:29,850 --> 00:00:36,280
Bird migration has been studied over many centuries through a variety of observations.

8
00:00:36,280 --> 00:00:42,425
But until relatively recently, where birds went to in the winter was considered something

9
00:00:42,425 --> 00:00:43,530
of a mystery.

10
00:00:43,530 --> 00:00:51,413
The lack of modern science and technology led to many theories that we now recognize as error-filled

11
00:00:51,413 --> 00:00:53,940
and even somewhat amusing.

12
00:00:53,940 --> 00:01:00,758
Take hibernation theory for example – two thousand years ago, it was commonly believed

13
00:01:00,758 --> 00:01:06,393
that when birds left an area, they went underwater to hibernate in the seas

14
00:01:06,393 --> 00:01:08,960
and oceans.

15
00:01:08,960 --> 00:01:15,136
Another theory for the regular appearance and disappearance of birds was that they spent

16
00:01:15,136 --> 00:01:20,470
winter hidden in mud till the weather changed and food became abundant again.

17
00:01:20,470 --> 00:01:27,745
The theory that some birds hibernate persisted until experiments were done on caged birds

18
00:01:27,745 --> 00:01:34,740
in the 1940s which demonstrated that birds have no hibernation instinct.

19
00:01:34,740 --> 00:01:42,014
One of the earliest naturalists and philosophers from ancient Greece was Aristotle who was

20
00:01:42,014 --> 00:01:48,366
the first writer to discuss the disappearance and reappearance of some bird species at

21
00:01:48,366 --> 00:01:50,290
certain times of year.

22
00:01:50,290 --> 00:01:58,995
He developed the theory of transmutation, the seasonal change of one species into another, by observing redstarts

23
00:01:58,995 --> 00:02:00,540
and robins.

24
00:02:00,540 --> 00:02:06,475
He observed that in the autumn, small birds called ‘redstarts’ began to lose their

25
00:02:06,475 --> 00:02:07,017
feathers,

26
00:02:07,017 --> 00:02:12,250
which convinced Aristotle that they changed into robins for the winter,

27
00:02:12,250 --> 00:02:16,100
and back into redstarts in the summer.

28
00:02:16,100 --> 00:02:22,558
These assumptions are understandable given that this pair of species are similar in shape,

29
00:02:22,558 --> 00:02:30,480
but are a classic example of an incorrect interpretation based on correct observations.

30
00:02:30,480 --> 00:02:36,882
The most bizarre theory was put forward by an English amateur scientist, Charles Morton,

31
00:02:36,882 --> 00:02:39,150
in the seventeenth century.

32
00:02:39,150 --> 00:02:45,793
He wrote a surprisingly well-regarded paper claiming that birds migrate to the moon

33
00:02:45,793 --> 00:02:47,680
and back every year.

34
00:02:47,680 --> 00:02:53,655
He came to this conclusion as the only logical explanation for the total disappearance

35
00:02:53,655 --> 00:02:59,530
of some species.

36
00:02:59,530 --> 00:03:05,086
One of the key moments in the development of migration theory came in 1822

37
00:03:05,086 --> 00:03:09,940
when a white stork was shot in Germany.

38
00:03:09,940 --> 00:03:16,517
This particular stork made history because of the long spear in its neck which

39
00:03:16,517 --> 00:03:25,100
incredibly had not killed it – everyone immediately realised this spear was definitely not European.

40
00:03:25,100 --> 00:03:29,710
It turned out to be a spear from a tribe in Central Africa.

41
00:03:29,710 --> 00:03:34,623
This was a truly defining moment in the history of ornithology

42
00:03:34,623 --> 00:03:41,340
because it was the first evidence that storks spend their winters in sub-Saharan Africa.

43
00:03:41,340 --> 00:03:48,384
You can still see the ‘arrow stork’ in the Zoological Collection of the University

44
00:03:48,384 --> 00:03:50,310
of Rostock in Germany.

45
00:03:50,310 --> 00:03:58,646
People gradually became aware that European birds moved south in autumn and north in summer

46
00:03:58,646 --> 00:04:03,535
but didn’t know much about it until the practice of catching birds

47
00:04:03,535 --> 00:04:06,380
and putting rings on their legs became established.

48
00:04:06,380 --> 00:04:14,012
Before this, very little information was available about the actual destinations of particular species

49
00:04:14,012 --> 00:04:15,860
and how they travelled there.

50
00:04:15,860 --> 00:04:22,662
People speculated that larger birds provided a kind of taxi service for smaller birds

51
00:04:22,662 --> 00:04:25,090
by carrying them on their backs.

52
00:04:25,090 --> 00:04:31,556
This idea came about because it seemed impossible that small birds weighing only a

53
00:04:31,556 --> 00:04:34,870
few grams could fly over vast oceans.

54
00:04:34,870 --> 00:04:41,580
This idea was supported by observations of bird behaviour such as the harassment of

55
00:04:41,580 --> 00:04:43,730
larger birds by smaller birds.

56
00:04:43,730 --> 00:04:53,744
The development of bird ringing, by a Danish schoolteacher, Hans Christian Cornelius Mortensen, made

57
00:04:53,744 --> 00:04:55,430
many discoveries possible.

58
00:04:55,430 --> 00:05:01,689
This is still common practice today and relies upon what is known as ‘recovery’

59
00:05:01,689 --> 00:05:06,082
– this is when ringed birds are found dead in the place they have

60
00:05:06,082 --> 00:05:06,904
migrated to,

61
00:05:06,904 --> 00:05:08,950
and identified.

62
00:05:08,950 --> 00:05:14,203
Huge amounts of data were gathered in the early part of the twentieth century

63
00:05:14,203 --> 00:05:20,098
and for the first time in history people understood where birds actually went to

64
00:05:20,098 --> 00:05:21,000
in winter.

65
00:05:21,000 --> 00:05:28,473
In 1931, an atlas was published showing where the most common species of European

66
00:05:28,473 --> 00:05:30,180
birds migrated to.

67
00:05:30,180 --> 00:05:35,000
More recent theories about bird migration …
